Brahim invests \$2500 at an annual interest rate of 6.75\% compounded monthly. Determine, algebraically, how many years it will take for his investment to reach an amount of \$10 500.

Use the formula:
\[
A=P\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)^{n t}
\]
where
\[
\begin{aligned}
& A=\text { the amount of the investment after } t \text { years } \\
& P=\text { the principal of the investment } \\
& r=\text { the annual interest rate (as a decimal) } \\
& n=\text { the number of compounding periods per year } \\
& t=\text { the length of the investment in years }
\end{aligned}
\]

\section*{Solution}
\[
\begin{aligned}
10500 & =2500\left(1+\frac{0.0675}{12}\right)^{12 t} \\
4.2 & =(1.005625)^{12 t} \\
\log 4.2 & =12 t \log 1.005625 \\
\frac{\log 4.2}{12 \log 1.005625} & =t \\
21.320250 & =t \\
21.320 \text { years } & =t
\end{aligned}
\]
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for substitution
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for simplification
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for applying logarithms
1 mark for power law
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for evaluating quotient of logarithms
